  {
    "statement": "The effectiveness of different sown floral mixes on agricultural land for wild bees shows varied outcomes depending on the mix composition and the bee species.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "statement": "Bumblebees in Europe have been documented foraging on field margins sown with agricultural nectar and pollen seed mixes.",
    "citations": [
      "18"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Field margins sown with perennial leguminous flowering plants attracted significantly more foraging bumblebees than naturally regenerated, grassy, or cropped field margins.",
    "citations": [
      "18"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Agricultural forage plant mixes that include legumes attracted greater numbers of bumblebees than perennial wildflower mixes, at least in the first year.",
    "citations": [
      "18"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "In UK trials, margins sown with agricultural legume plants degraded in value to bumblebees.",
    "citations": [
      "18"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Margins sown with agricultural legume plants would need re-sowing every few years to maintain their value for bumblebees.",
    "citations": [
      "18"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Substantial numbers of wild bees have been recorded in Europe and North America foraging on perennial or annual sown flowering plants in agricultural environments.",
    "citations": [
      "17"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Patches with high proportions of phacelia attracted substantial numbers of foraging wild bees, particularly bumblebees.",
    "citations": [
      "17"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "The number of wild bee species foraging on patches with high proportions of phacelia ranged from eight to 35.",
    "citations": [
      "17"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Uncropped field margins in the UK sown with wild flowers supported a higher abundance of foraging bumblebees than cropped field edges, grassy margins, or naturally regenerated margins.",
    "citations": [
      "19"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Uncropped field margins in the UK sown with wild flowers supported a higher species richness of foraging bumblebees than cropped field edges, grassy margins, or naturally regenerated margins.",
    "citations": [
      "19"
    ]
  },
  {
    "statement": "Perennial leguminous herbs in seed mixtures are important forage sources for bumblebees, particularly for long-tongued species.",
    "citations": [
